ck from expanding vertically and contests the center control. It is a solid, standard defensive move.\n2.  **I9 (Diagonal Move):** This places a stone diagonally adjacent to H8. It is also a common opening response, aiming to complicate the game and look for diagonal connections, but it leaves the orthogonal lines open for Black.\n3.  **I8 (Horizontal Block):** Similar to H9, this blocks Black horizontally. It is effectively equivalent to H9 in terms of strategic value at this stage.\n\nReason:\nSince Black holds the initiative with the first move at the center, White\'s best course of action is to defend by placing a stone directly next to Black\'s. H9 is a strong choice because it blocks the vertical axis and keeps the game tight, preventing Black from easily creating open threes or fours in the early game. It allows White to pivot to offense once the immediate center control is neutralized.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "H", "row": 9}'}
